facts:
If the result equals the point's $y$-value, the point **satisfies** the equation and lies on the line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.6,0) -- (4.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-0.4,0.2) -- (3.4,3.05);
\node[right] at (3.55,3.05) {$y=mx+c$};
\fill (2,2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above left] at (2,2) {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

If the result does not equal the point's $y$-value, the point does not lie on the line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.6,0) -- (4.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-0.4,0.2) -- (3.4,3.05);
\node[right] at (3.55,3.05) {$y=mx+c$};
\fill (2.6,1.2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[below right] at (2.6,1.2) {$Q$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

